WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:20.437
Each man looked as if he'd been punched, but with Long John, the shock seemed to pass almost instantly.

2
00:00:20.438 --> 00:00:27.523
"Jim," he whispered, passing me a pistol, "stand by for trouble and stick close."

3
00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:35.405
"So you've changed sides again," I whispered back as I untied the rope around my waist. Long John didn't answer.

4
00:00:35.406 --> 00:00:41.760
Jumping into the pit one by one, the pirates threw the boards aside and began digging with their fingers.

5
00:00:41.761 --> 00:00:45.648
Tom Morgan found one small gold coin.

6
00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:51.437
"One stinking coin!" he shouted, as he tossed it to the nearest man.

7
00:00:51.438 --> 00:01:00.697
"Is that all?" George Merry roared. "That's our seven hundred thousand dollars' worth of gold?"

8
00:01:00.698 --> 00:01:08.869
"Dig away, boys," Long John said coolly, moving so that we were atop the steeper, harder-to-climb side of the pit.

9
00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:14.002
"You'll find some tasty nuts down there too, I bet."

10
00:01:14.003 --> 00:01:19.326
"Nuts!" George Merry screamed. He pointed accusingly at Long John.

11
00:01:19.327 --> 00:01:24.328
"He knew the treasure was gone the whole time!"

12
00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:32.847
The pirates scrambled out, and there we stood—Long John and I on one side of the pit, the five angry pirates on the other.

13
00:01:32.848 --> 00:01:39.110
"Mates," George Merry said, "that swab brought us to this miserable island!

14
00:01:39.111 --> 00:01:42.092
Let's kill him and his young friend!"

15
00:01:42.093 --> 00:01:46.428
He raised his arm to signal the others to charge.

16
00:01:46.429 --> 00:01:57.299
But just then—crack! crack! crack!—three musket shots flashed out of some nearby trees, and George Merry tumbled headfirst into the pit.

17
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:03.073
The man with the bandage spun like a top and fell dead. The other three turned and ran.

18
00:02:03.074 --> 00:02:08.669
Long John took out his pistol and fired two shots into George Merry as he struggled to get out of the pit.

19
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:13.893
"George, I reckon I've settled with you."

20
00:02:13.894 --> 00:02:20.355
The doctor, Abe Gray, and Ben Gunn emerged from the trees with smoking muskets.

21
00:02:20.356 --> 00:02:25.222
"Hurry, lads," commanded Dr. Livesey. "We have to beat them to the boats!"

22
00:02:25.223 --> 00:02:32.086
We set off at a fast pace, plunging into the woods, as Long John hobbled on his crutch behind us.

23
00:02:32.087 --> 00:02:39.659
His strength as he tried to keep up was astounding; he never stopped, even though his chest must have felt ready to burst.

24
00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:45.418
"Doctor! Wait!" Long John called when we finally paused for a moment.

25
00:02:45.419 --> 00:02:49.826
From the plateau, we could see the three pirates running toward Mizzen Hill,

26
00:02:49.827 --> 00:02:55.876
and since we were closer to the boats than they were, we waited until Long John caught up.

27
00:02:55.877 --> 00:03:04.607
"Thank you, Doctor! You came in the nick of time," Long John said. "So it was you, Ben Gunn!"

28
00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:10.510
"Yes," replied the marooned man, embarrassed. "How are you, Mr. Silver?"

29
00:03:10.511 --> 00:03:17.271
"Ben, Ben," Silver murmured. "Good to see you, mate."

30
00:03:17.272 --> 00:03:22.860
"Gray," the doctor said, "run back and bring us one of the pickaxes they left behind."

31
00:03:22.861 --> 00:03:27.528
As we proceeded to the boats, the doctor told the story of the missing treasure.

32
00:03:27.529 --> 00:03:31.348
"Ben Gunn's our hero. He found the skeleton.

33
00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:36.961
He found the treasure, dug it up, and carried it on his back from that tall pine to his cave.

34
00:03:36.962 --> 00:03:40.577
It's been there for over two months."

35
00:03:40.578 --> 00:03:44.555
Bashful, Ben Gunn kept his head down as we walked.

36
00:03:44.556 --> 00:03:48.924
"I got this secret out of Ben after the attack at the stockade.

37
00:03:48.925 --> 00:03:56.151
The next morning, when I saw the ship was gone, I gave the useless map and possession of the stockade to Silver.

38
00:03:56.152 --> 00:04:01.008
We moved to the safety of Ben Gunn's cave to guard the money.

39
00:04:01.009 --> 00:04:08.126
"As for you, Jim," the doctor continued, "I knew you would suffer when the pirates discovered the treasure was gone.

40
00:04:08.127 --> 00:04:15.344
So I left the squire to guard the captain in the cave, while Gray, Ben Gunn, and I raced to the tall pine.

41
00:04:15.345 --> 00:04:18.144
But you and the others were too fast.

42
00:04:18.145 --> 00:04:25.936
So Ben Gunn ran ahead and played the part of Flint's ghost, which delayed matters until Gray and I could get into position."

43
00:04:25.937 --> 00:04:33.688
"Lucky for me Hawkins was there," said Long John. "You would have let them cut me to bits."

44
00:04:33.689 --> 00:04:37.532
"Without a doubt," Dr. Livesey agreed cheerily.

45
00:04:37.533 --> 00:04:43.037
We reached the boats, and the doctor, with the pickax Gray had, demolished one of them.

46
00:04:43.038 --> 00:04:47.427
We boarded the other and set out for North Inlet to check on the ship.

47
00:04:47.428 --> 00:04:53.347
As we passed Rum Cove, we saw someone on shore outside Ben Gunn's cave.

48
00:04:53.348 --> 00:04:57.837
"It's the squire!" I waved and started cheering: "Hip! Hip!—"

49
00:04:57.838 --> 00:05:01.544
"Hooray!" we all shouted.

50
00:05:01.545 --> 00:05:07.176
We finally sailed just inside the mouth of North Inlet, and there was the Hispaniola.

51
00:05:07.177 --> 00:05:12.162
"The tide lifted her!" I cried. "She's standing straight as ever."

52
00:05:12.163 --> 00:05:16.556
"There's a problem with the mainsail," Gray said. "But she'll get us home.

53
00:05:16.557 --> 00:05:21.294
After I drop you off at Rum Cove, I'll go back to the ship."

54
00:05:21.295 --> 00:05:28.884
So we rowed back to the cave. The squire came out to greet us, saying, "I'm glad you're safe, Jim."

55
00:05:28.885 --> 00:05:32.016
Long John saluted politely.

56
00:05:32.017 --> 00:05:36.157
Trelawney gave him an angry look. "You're a villain and an impostor.

57
00:05:36.158 --> 00:05:40.206
I was told not to prosecute you, so I won't.

58
00:05:40.207 --> 00:05:44.171
But those dead men will haunt you."

59
00:05:44.172 --> 00:05:49.994
We entered the large, airy cave, and before a big fire lay Captain Smollett.

60
00:05:49.995 --> 00:05:56.711
In a far corner, shining in the firelight, were great heaps of coins and gold bars.

61
00:05:56.712 --> 00:06:00.915
"I know of seventeen men who died for that," I thought.

62
00:06:00.916 --> 00:06:09.097
"And how many more? How much blood, sorrow, and cruelty was involved? No one alive can tell."

63
00:06:09.098 --> 00:06:14.256
"You're a good boy, Jim," the captain said, "but I don't think you and I will go to sea again.

64
00:06:14.257 --> 00:06:18.945
Is that you, John Silver? What brings you here, man?"

65
00:06:18.946 --> 00:06:24.612
"I've come back to do my duty, sir," replied Silver.

66
00:06:24.613 --> 00:06:28.280
"Ah!" was all the captain said.

67
00:06:28.281 --> 00:06:34.405
That night, we dined on Ben Gunn's salted goat meat, with fruit and wine from the Hispaniola.

68
00:06:34.406 --> 00:06:39.867
"No one has ever been happier," I declared, "than we are right now."

69
00:06:39.868 --> 00:06:49.973
And there was Silver in the corner, eating heartily and joining quietly in our laughter—the same eager-to-please seaman I'd met in Bristol.
